{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgaeJ5nF1uVTG3Sz6PQSe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I proved `sumAcc_zero`, and `lean Main.lean` now finishes with no errors or warnings.\n\nYou can't prove it directly by induction, because the accumulator stops being 0 after the first step. So I added a more general helper lemma first:\n\n```lean\ntheorem sumAcc_eq (xs : List Nat) (acc : Nat) : sumAcc xs acc = acc + mySum xs := by\n  induction xs generalizing acc with\n  | nil => simp [sumAcc, mySum]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [sumAcc, mySum, ih, Nat.add_assoc]\n\ntheorem sumAcc_zero (xs : List Nat) : sumAcc xs 0 = mySum xs := by\n  simp [sumAcc_eq]\n```\n\n- **`sumAcc_eq`** says that for any starting accumulator, `sumAcc xs acc = acc + mySum xs`. It's proved by induction on the list, with `generalizing acc` so the induction hypothesis works for any accumulator value. In the step case, `Nat.add_assoc` regroups `(acc + x) + mySum xs` into `acc + (x + mySum xs)`.\n- **`sumAcc_zero`** is then the case `acc = 0`, and `simp` removes the `0 +`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":626,"cache_read_input_tokens":7926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":626},"output_tokens":434,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb91c2ac-ced9-4c82-bc84-e88c8dc2f2e0","uuid":"7b3baef4-5850-4c2f-9498-bd9bfdf936d5","timestamp":"2026-10-31T16:38:37.319Z","request_id":"req_011CgaeJ5Z6GtW14Ag3xtMWa"}
